h  fresh blood and dried blood on the tips  using the elisa methods for both the  spike and the rbd  she did a lot of work devising the  optimal antigens to use  the constructs and so on and so forth  and eventually  came up with a very nice sensitive and  specific  elisa for both the spike and the rbd and  we used a method in which  we tested both igg and igm for both the  spike and the rbd  and in order to be determined as a true  positive you had to have  at least one spike and one rbd  antibody positive so either both iggs  both igms or a combination of one igg  and one igm  and this gave us a really nice  specificity and working with our  statisticians  we were able to devise the optimal  cutoffs for these assays  so that we were really getting a nice  specific result  uh and and ideally not calling anyone  who wasn't truly a positive a positive  while at the same time not losing too  many true positives  as false negatives  and i should mention those those methods  were published  uh previously in nature communications  uh  in mid 2020  so what happened with this zero survey  that we did so  we actually were able to identify 462  almost 463 thousand volunteers  so this was through advertising press  release  we had an email set up in which people  responded as well as phone calls  saying they were interested in  participating in the study so we were  just amazed at how great a response we  had and how many people were interested  in participating in this study  now we didn't need that many people that  was way more than we could possibly test  but but this gave us a really nice base  to work with  and so what we did was because we wanted  to access the entire country and get a  very representative sample  we sent all of those individuals a  simple demographic screening question  so questions like what's your race  what's your ethnicity your age your sex  where do you live and so on and so forth  we had 241 000 people respond to that  demographic survey  so now